<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="linear-combs" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Linear independence</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A version of this activity appears in
      <url
          href="https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/sec-linear-dep.html#sec-linear-dep">
        Understanding Linear Algebra
      </url>
      by David Austin.
    </p>

  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Strengthen the connection between questions about linear
          combinations and questions about the consistency of linear
          systems.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Develop a geometric condition that explains when the span of
          a set of vectors is the same as the span of a proper subsect
          of those vectors.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Determine how the reduced row echelon form of a matrix
          explains when the span of the columns is the same as the
          span of a proper subset of those columns.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="linear-comb-subsection">
    <title>Some background</title>

    <p>
      At this point of the course, students have encountered linear
      combinations of vectors and the concept of the span of a set of
      vectors.  As a reminder, an example of a linear combination of
      vectors <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m> is
      <md>
        2\vvec - 3\wvec
      </md>.
      Asking whether a given vector is a linear combination
      of a set of vectors is the same as asking about the consistency
      of an associated linear system.
    </p>

    <p>
      Also, the <term>span</term> of a set of vectors
      is the set of all linear combinations that can be formed from the
      vectors.
    </p>

    <p>
      The next activity provides a <q>Sage cell</q> as a way for
      students to explore.  For thos unfamiliar with Sage, some
      background is provided below.
    </p>

    <proof>
      <title>Sage background</title>

      <p>
        Given vectors <m>\vvec=\twovec23</m> and
        <m>\wvec=\twovec{-3}4</m>, we can ask whether
        <m>\twovec{13}{-6}</m> is a linear combination of these vectors
        by constructing the augmented matrix and finding its reduced row
        echelon form, such as
        <md>
          \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}
          2 \amp -3 \amp 13 \\
          3 \amp 4 \amp -6 \\
          \end{array}\right]
          \sim
          \left[\begin{array}{cc|c}
          1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
          0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
          \end{array}\right]
        </md>,
        which tells us that
        <md>
          \twovec{13}{-6} =2\vvec - 3\wvec
        </md>.
        Sage can perform the row reduction as follows:
      </p>

      <sage>
        <input>
          A = matrix([[2,-3,13],[3,4,-6]])
          A.rref()
        </input>
      </sage>
    </proof>
  </subsection>

  <worksheet>
    <title>Introduction to linear independence</title>

    <activity>

      <introduction>
        <p>We will begin by exploring the span of two sets of
        vectors using the following Sage cell.</p>
        <sage>
          <input>
          </input>
        </sage>
      </introduction>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the set of vectors
            <md>\vvec_1=\threevec0{-1}2,~~~~
            \vvec_2=\threevec31{-1},~~~~
            \vvec_3=\threevec201</md>.
            Is it possible to write the vector
            <m>\threevec310</m> as a linear combination of these
            vectors?  If so, how can it be written as a linear combination?
          </p>

          <p>
            How about the vector <m>\threevec311</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>Express these results by writing a sentence or two using
          the concept of <em>span</em>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the reduced row echelon form of the matrix <m>A</m> whose
            columns are <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and
            <m>\vvec_3</m>:
            <md>
              A = \begin{bmatrix}\vvec_1 \amp \vvec_2 \amp \vvec_3
              \end{bmatrix}
            </md>.
            What does this tell us about the span of
            the set of these vectors; in particular, is every vector
            in <m>\real^3</m> in the span of this set of vectors?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            We now consider the set of vectors
            <md>\wvec_1=\threevec0{-1}2,~~~~
            \wvec_2=\threevec31{-1},~~~~
            \wvec_3=\threevec301</md>.
            Is it possible to write the vector
            <m>\threevec310</m> as a linear combination of these
            vectors?  If so, how can it be written as a linear combination?
          </p>

          <p>
            How about the vector <m>\threevec311</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the reduced row echelon form of the matrix <m>B</m> whose
            columns are <m>\wvec_1</m>, <m>\wvec_2</m>, and
            <m>\wvec_3</m>:
            <md>
              B = \begin{bmatrix}\vvec_1 \amp \vvec_2 \amp \vvec_3
              \end{bmatrix}
            </md>.
            What does this tell us about the span of
            the set of these vectors; in particular, is every vector
            in <m>\real^3</m> in the span of this set of vectors?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            We have now considered two sets of three vectors in
            <m>\real^3</m>.  In one case, the span is <m>\real^3</m>
            and in the other case, it is not.  We would like to
            understand the difference in these two examples.
          </p>

          <p>
            Are you able to write <m>\vvec_3</m> as a linear
            combination of <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.  How
            does the reduced row echelon form of <m>A</m> help us
            answer this question?
          </p>

          <p>
            Are you able to write <m>\wvec_3</m> as a linear
            combination of <m>\wvec_1</m> and <m>\wvec_2</m>.  How
            does the reduced row echelon form of <m>B</m> help us
            answer this question?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why a linear combination of the vectors
            <m>\wvec_1</m>, <m>\wvec_2</m>, and <m>\wvec_3</m> can be
            rewritten as a linear combination of <m>\wvec_1</m> and
            <m>\wvec_2</m> alone.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain why
            <md>
              \laspan{\wvec_1,\wvec_2,\wvec_3} =
              \laspan{\wvec_1,\wvec_2}
            </md>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </activity>
  </worksheet>
</section>
